Minimal threshold fix for MALLM, against the release base a57c4a77d37f (v1.0.6, 2026-06-15).

WHY. `ThresholdConsensus.make_decision` decides consensus from the position of the most recent entry
carrying a non-empty `solution`. That encoding was paired with `Agent.improve()` writing `solution=""`
when an agent agrees (commit a26495a, 2024-09-30 — the predicate and the writer were changed together
and are equivalent). Commit e20d40c (2024-10-16, "consensus voting") changed the writer's agreeing
branch to `agreements[-1].solution` without changing the predicate. Since then every entry carries a
solution, so `num_agreements` is always 1 and the threshold cannot be met for any threshold above 1/n.
Every tagged release from v1.0.0 (2024-11-09) onward is after that commit.

WHAT. One line. The predicate reads the per-agent stance boolean again, which is the semantics of the
author's own commit 24567a0 (2024-07-21) and the semantics the paper describes.

WHY NOT THE OTHER DIRECTION. Restoring `improve()` to write `""` would also work, but
`current_agreement.solution` is returned as the delivered answer (consensus.py:95,103), so changing the
writer changes which solution is returned. Patching the predicate leaves the writer, and therefore the
output, exactly as the release has it.

SCOPE. This is our variant, not the published artifact. It is labelled as such everywhere, the
unmodified release is retained as the comparison, and the whole-version difference is not attributed to
this single line.

--- a/mallm/decision_protocols/consensus.py
+++ b/mallm/decision_protocols/consensus.py
@@ -64,7 +64,7 @@
             (
                 (i, agreement)
                 for i, agreement in enumerate(reversed_agreements, 1)
-                if agreement.solution
+                if not agreement.agreement
             ),
             (None, None),
         )
